ective and other treatment options may be needed, highlighting a key decision point driven by this community’s core mechanism. [Data: Entities (3771, 5154); Relationships (11378)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Iodine uptake is the enabling mechanism for RAI-based evaluation and therapy in certain thyroid cancers\",\"explanation\":\"IODINE UPTAKE is defined as the phenomenon of thyroid cancer or metastases absorbing iodine, and it is explicitly linked to why whole-body RAI imaging may be helpful (e.g., metastases that took up iodine). This makes iodine uptake a pivotal capability/attribute that can determine whether RAI-based approaches are likely to work. [Data: Entities (5154, 3771); Relationships (11378)]\\n\\nThe relationship record describes iodine uptake as central to RAI-based evaluation and treatment, reinforcing that this is not merely a biological observation but an operational determinant in clinical workflows (imaging selection and therapeutic planning). [Data: Relationships (11378)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Non-uptake of iodine implies reduced effectiveness of RAI and a need for alternative options\",\"explanation\":\"The iodine entity description states that when tumors do not take up iodine, RAI is less effective and other treatment options may be needed. This frames iodine uptake status as a practical branching point in patient management, affecting expected utility of RAI-based imaging and treatment. [Data: Entities (3771)]\\n\\nWithin the community structure, this creates a clear dependency: the value of RAI-based approaches is conditional on the presence of IODINE UPTAKE, tying clinical impact to the uptake phenomenon rather than iodine exposure alone. [Data: Entities (3771, 5154); Relationships (11378)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Iodized salt is a cited dietary source of iodine, linking nutrition/public health to the community\",\"explanation\":\"IODIZED SALT is defined as salt fortified with iodine and is cited as a dietary source of iodine. The explicit relationship indica